window.SN_INDEX=[{"i": "excel-01", "t": "Getting Started with Excel for Data Analysis", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-01-getting-started-with-excel-for-data-analysis.html", "s": " Why Excel still matters in 2026 Even with Python, SQL, Power BI and AI assistants, Excel is still the first tool almost every analyst opens. It is where business teams keep budgets, targets, trackers and exports from other systems. If you can clean, analyse and present data in Excel quickly, you can add value from day one in any data role. Excel is also the easiest place to learn data thinking. Every idea you meet here comes back later: Idea in Excel Same idea later in the course A table with one header row A SQL table, a pandas DataFrame, a Power BI table SUMIFS, COUNTIFS GROUP BY in SQL, groupby() in pandas, DAX measures VLOOKUP / XLOOKUP SQL JOIN , pandas merge() , Power BI relationships Pivot table SQL aggregation, pivot_table() in pandas, Power BI matrix Conditional formatting Highlighting in dashboards Live class What was covered in class Live class 1 was the programme introduction. Live class 2 covered the basics of Excel, terminology, shortcuts, Excel properties, data types, data validation and formatting, and we started working on the first sheet. This lesson covers the first half; data validation and formatting continue in Lessons 2 and 3. The Excel window, part by part The main parts of the Excel window. The active cell is C4, and the formula bar shows the formula inside it. Part What it does Tip Ribbon Commands grouped into tabs: Home, Insert, Formulas, Data, Review, View Press Alt to see a key letter on every command Name Box Shows the address of the active cell"}, {"i": "excel-02", "t": "Data Entry, Data Validation & Data Cleaning", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-02-data-entry-data-validation-and-data-cleaning.html", "s": " Garbage in, garbage out Most time in real analytics is not spent on fancy charts; it is spent fixing data: extra spaces, inconsistent spellings ( Bangalore , Bengaluru , bengaluru ), numbers stored as text, duplicates and blanks. Surveys of data teams regularly report that cleaning and preparing data takes well over half of an analyst's time. There are two ways to win this fight: Prevent problems at entry time, and clean whatever slips through. Both are needed. Fast, consistent data entry AutoFill and the fill handle Select a cell and drag the small square at its bottom-right corner (the fill handle ). Excel continues the pattern it recognises. You type Drag gives you Jan Feb, Mar, Apr … Monday Tuesday, Wednesday … 1 , 2 (select both) 3, 4, 5 … Q1 Q2, Q3, Q4, Q1 … 01-04-2026 02-04-2026, 03-04-2026 … (right-drag for months or years) Item 1 Item 2, Item 3 … =B2*12 =B3*12 , =B4*12 … Double-click the fill handle to fill a formula down as far as the column next to it has data. This is the fastest way to copy a formula to 10,000 rows. Tip Custom lists File → Options → Advanced → Edit Custom Lists lets you add your own sequences, for example North, South, East, West or your company's department order. AutoFill and custom sort then follow that order. Flash Fill (Ctrl + E) Flash Fill learns a pattern from one or two examples you type. It is perfect for splitting names, extracting codes or reformatting text. X Flash Fill: type the first answer in each column, then press Ctrl + E A B C"}, {"i": "excel-03", "t": "Number, Custom & Conditional Formatting", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-03-number-custom-and-conditional-formatting.html", "s": " Formatting changes how a value looks, not what it is This is the most important idea in this lesson. When you format 0.256 as 26% , the cell still contains 0.256 . Every formula that uses the cell sees 0.256 . X Same stored value, different formats A B C 1 Stored value Format applied What you see 2 0.256 Percentage, 0 decimals 26% 3 0.256 Percentage, 1 decimal 25.6% 4 1,234,568 Number with separator 1,234,567.89 5 46139 Date 27-Apr-2026 6 46139 Long date Monday, 27 April 2026 7 0.6875 Time 16:30 C2 = TEXT (A2, &quot;0%&quot; ) C3 = TEXT (A3, &quot;0.0%&quot; ) C4 = TEXT (A4, &quot;#,##0.00&quot; ) C5 = TEXT (A5, &quot;dd-mmm-yyyy&quot; ) C6 = TEXT (A6, &quot;dddd, d mmmm yyyy&quot; ) C7 = TEXT (A7, &quot;hh:mm&quot; ) Column C uses the TEXT function to show exactly what each format produces. ( TEXT returns text, so use it for labels, not for numbers you still need to calculate with.) Watch out Rounding by format vs rounding by formula Formatting 2.456 with 1 decimal shows 2.5 but keeps 2.456 . Adding three such cells can show 7.4 while the visible numbers add up to 7.5 . If the rounded value must be used in calculations (invoices, tax), round it with a formula: =ROUND(A2,1) . Cell formatting the professional way Open Format Cells with Ctrl + 1 . It has six tabs: Number, Alignment, Font, Border, Fill, Protection. Goal How Bold header with fill colour Home → Bold ( Ctrl + B ) and Fill Color, or Cell Styles → Heading Wrap long text Home → Wrap Text ( Alt + H, W ) Centre a title"}, {"i": "excel-04", "t": "Formulas, Cell References & Aggregate Functions", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-04-formulas-cell-references-and-aggregate-functions.html", "s": " Formula vs function Definition Two words people mix up A formula is any calculation that starts with = . It can use cell references, numbers, operators and functions: =B2*C2 , =(B2-C2)/C2 , =SUM(B2:B10)*1.18 . A function is a named, ready-made calculation inside a formula: SUM , AVERAGE , IF . A function has a name, brackets and arguments separated by commas. Anatomy of a function call. Arguments in square brackets are optional. When you type a function name and ( , Excel shows a tooltip with its syntax, for example SUMIFS(sum_range, criteria_range1, criteria1, [criteria_range2, criteria2], …) . Press Ctrl + A after the bracket to open the Function Arguments dialog, which explains every argument and previews the result. Operators and order of calculation Operator Meaning Example Result + - * / Add, subtract, multiply, divide =10+4*2 18 ^ Power =2^10 1024 % Percent =500*18% 90 &amp; Join text =\"Q\"&amp;2 Q2 = &lt;&gt; &gt; &lt; &gt;= &lt;= Comparison, returns TRUE/FALSE =B2&gt;=100 TRUE or FALSE : Range A1:A10 10 cells , Union (inside functions) =SUM(A1:A3,C1:C3) Adds both ranges Excel follows the usual maths order, remembered as BODMAS/PEMDAS : brackets, then ^ , then * and / , then + and - , then &amp; , then comparisons. X Order of operations A B C 1 Formula Result Why 2 =10+4*2 18 Multiply first: 10 + 8 3 =(10+4)*2 28 Brackets first: 14 × 2 4 =2^3^2 64 Excel works left to right: (2^3)^2 = 64 5 =-2^2 4 Negation before power in Excel: (-2)^2 = 4 6 =100*(1+18%) 118 Add 18% GS"}, {"i": "excel-05", "t": "Text Functions, Logical Functions & IF", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-05-text-functions-logical-functions-and-if.html", "s": " Why text functions matter Real data arrives as text: order IDs like IN-2025-00042 , product codes, email addresses, \"Last, First\" names, addresses and free-text comments. Text functions let you pull out the part you need (the year, the region code, the domain) so you can analyse it. Measuring and extracting text Function What it returns Example Result LEN(text) Number of characters =LEN(\"Pune\") 4 LEFT(text, n) First n characters =LEFT(\"IN-2025-00042\",2) IN RIGHT(text, n) Last n characters =RIGHT(\"IN-2025-00042\",5) 00042 MID(text, start, n) n characters from position start =MID(\"IN-2025-00042\",4,4) 2025 FIND(find, text, [start]) Position of text (case-sensitive, no wildcards) =FIND(\"-\",\"IN-2025-00042\") 3 SEARCH(find, text, [start]) Position (not case-sensitive, wildcards allowed) =SEARCH(\"pune\",\"PUNE-411001\") 1 X Extracting parts of an order ID A B C D E F 1 Order ID Country =LEFT(A2,2) Year =MID(A2,4,4) Order no. =RIGHT(A2,5) Length Year as number 2 IN-2025-00042 IN 2025 00042 13 2025 3 IN-2023-01519 IN 2023 01519 13 2023 4 IN-2024-02988 IN 2024 02988 13 2024 B2 = LEFT (A2, 2 ) C2 = MID (A2, 4 , 4 ) D2 = RIGHT (A2, 5 ) E2 = LEN (A2) F2 = VALUE ( MID (A2, 4 , 4 )) Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Text functions always return text . MID(A2,4,4) returns the text \"2025\" , not the number 2025. Wrap with VALUE() (or multiply by 1) to get a number you can compare or add. When the position changes: FIND + MID Codes rarely have fixed lengths. Combi"}, {"i": "excel-06", "t": "Sorting, Filtering & Dynamic Arrays", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-06-sorting-filtering-and-dynamic-arrays.html", "s": " The sample table We will use this small sales table (sheet Reps , range A1:F9 ) so every result is easy to check by hand. Revenue is =D2*E2 . X Reps: sample sales table A B C D E F 1 Rep Region Product Units Price Revenue 2 Asha North Laptop 5 55,000 275,000 3 Ravi West Phone 12 18,000 216,000 4 Meera South Laptop 3 55,000 165,000 5 Kabir North Tablet 8 25,000 200,000 6 Zoya East Phone 15 18,000 270,000 7 Farhan West Laptop 4 55,000 220,000 8 Neha South Tablet 10 25,000 250,000 9 Rohan North Phone 9 18,000 162,000 F2 =D2*E2 Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Sorting Quick sort Click any cell in a column and use Data → Sort A to Z or Sort Z to A (or Home → Sort &amp; Filter). Excel detects the table around the cell and keeps each row together. Watch out Never sort a single selected column If you select only one column and sort, Excel asks whether to \"expand the selection\". Choosing \"Continue with the current selection\" sorts that column alone and scrambles your data: names no longer match their revenue. Always click a single cell inside the table, or select the whole table. Multi-level (level-wise) sort Data → Sort opens the Sort dialog, where you add levels. Excel sorts by the first level, then breaks ties with the next. The Sort dialog with two levels: Region A to Z, then Revenue largest to smallest. X Result: Region A→Z, then Revenue high→low A B C D 1 Rep Region Product Revenue 2 Zoya East Phone 270,000 3 Asha North Laptop 275,000 4 Kabir"}, {"i": "excel-07", "t": "Lookup Functions: VLOOKUP, XLOOKUP and INDEX-MATCH", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-07-lookup-functions-vlookup-xlookup-and-index-match.html", "s": " Why lookups matter Business data is split across tables: orders in one, products in another, customers in a third. A lookup fetches a value from one table using a key that both tables share, such as a product ID or employee ID. A lookup takes a key from one table, finds it in another table, and returns a value from the same row. Tip You are already learning SQL joins A lookup is a left join for one column at a time: keep every row of the main table and bring in matching information from the other table. You will see the same idea as JOIN in SQL (SQL Lesson 8), merge() in pandas, and relationships in Power BI. We will use this lookup table on a sheet called Prod : X Prod: product lookup table (A1:E7) A B C D E 1 Product ID Product Category Price Stock 2 P101 Office Chair Furniture 4500 32 3 P102 Standing Desk Furniture 18500 8 4 P103 Laser Printer Technology 12900 15 5 P104 Notebook Pack Office Supplies 240 540 6 P105 Smartphone Technology 21999 27 7 P106 Bookcase Furniture 7600 0 VLOOKUP =VLOOKUP(lookup_value, table_array, col_index_num, [range_lookup]) lookup_value : what you are looking for (the key). table_array : the table to search; the key must be in its first column . col_index_num : which column of that table to return (1 = first column). range_lookup : FALSE (or 0) for an exact match. Use TRUE only for approximate matching on sorted bands. X Order lines looking up product details with VLOOKUP A B C D E F 1 Order Product ID Qty Product =VLOOKUP(B2,Prod!$A$2:$E$7,2,FA"}, {"i": "excel-08", "t": "Pivot Tables from Basic to Advanced", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-08-pivot-tables-from-basic-to-advanced.html", "s": " What a pivot table does A pivot table groups rows by one or more fields and summarises a number for each group (sum, count, average…). It is the fastest way to answer \"how much, by what?\" questions, with no formulas. A pivot table turns thousands of order lines into a compact summary: rows grouped by Region, columns by Category, values summed. Creating a pivot table Step by step Step by step Make sure the data is structured (Lesson 1): one header row, no blank rows, no merged cells. Ideally convert it to a Table ( Ctrl + T ) so new rows are included automatically. Click any cell in the data → Insert → PivotTable (or Alt, N, V ). Choose New Worksheet → OK. An empty pivot appears with the PivotTable Fields pane on the right. Drag fields into the four areas: drag region to Rows , category to Columns , sales to Values . Right-click any number → Number Format → #,##0 so the format sticks when the pivot refreshes. The PivotTable Fields pane: a list of fields at the top, four areas at the bottom. Area What it does Example Rows One row per unique value Region, Category, Order Date Columns One column per unique value Year, Segment Values The numbers being summarised Sum of Sales, Count of Order ID Filters Filter the whole pivot by a field Segment = Corporate The pivot cache, Refresh and Refresh All A pivot table does not read your sheet directly. When you create it, Excel copies the data into a hidden pivot cache , and the pivot reads from that cache. This is why pivots are fast, and"}, {"i": "excel-09", "t": "Date, Time & Number Functions and Named Ranges", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-09-date-time-and-number-functions-and-named-ranges.html", "s": " Dates and times are numbers Excel stores a date as the number of days since 1 January 1900 (that day is 1). A time is a fraction of a day : 12:00 noon is 0.5 and 06:00 is 0.25. A date with a time is simply a whole number plus a fraction. X What is really stored A B C 1 You see Stored as Explanation 2 27-04-2026 46139 Days since 1-Jan-1900 3 12:00 12:00 Half a day 4 06:30 0.2708 6.5 hours ÷ 24 5 27-04-2026 18:00 46139.75 Date + time B2 =A2 B3 = TIME ( 12 , 0 , 0 ) B4 = TIME ( 6 , 30 , 0 ) B5 = DATE ( 2026 , 4 , 27 )+ TIME ( 18 , 0 , 0 ) This is why date arithmetic works: X Date arithmetic A B C D E 1 Order date Ship date Days to ship =B2-A2 Due date (+30 days) Overdue? (as of 15-Jun-2026) 2 27-04-2026 02-05-2026 5 27-05-2026 Overdue 3 20-05-2026 21-05-2026 1 19-06-2026 OK 4 01-06-2026 08-06-2026 7 01-07-2026 OK C2 =B2-A2 D2 =A2+ 30 E2 = IF ( DATE ( 2026 , 6 , 15 )&gt;D2, &quot;Overdue&quot; , &quot;OK&quot; ) Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Tip TODAY() and NOW() =TODAY() returns today's date and =NOW() the date and time. They are volatile : they recalculate every time the workbook changes, so results move every day. For reports that must not change, type the \"as of\" date into a cell and refer to it, as in the overdue example above. Shortcuts: Ctrl + ; types today's date as a fixed value. Building and taking apart dates Function Returns Example (A2 = 27-04-2026) Result DATE(y, m, d) A date from parts =DATE(2026,4,27) 27-04-2026 YEAR , MONTH"}, {"i": "excel-10", "t": "Charts and Advanced Charts", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-10-charts-and-advanced-charts.html", "s": " Start with the question, not the chart Choose a chart by the question you are answering. Creating and editing a chart Step by step The fast way Select the data including headers (or a summary table / pivot table). For a quick chart press Alt + F1 (embedded) or F11 (new chart sheet). Or use Insert → Recommended Charts to preview options. Click the chart: three buttons appear at its top-right: Chart Elements (+), Chart Styles (brush) and Chart Filters (funnel). Chart Design tab: Switch Row/Column, Select Data, Change Chart Type, Move Chart, Quick Layout. Format pane: double-click any element (axis, series, label) or press Ctrl + 1 with it selected. Element Good practice Title Say the insight: \"Technology drives 77% of sales\", not \"Sales by Category\" Axes Start bar/column axes at zero; format numbers (K, L, %); remove unnecessary decimals Gridlines Light grey or none Data labels Use them instead of an axis when there are few bars Legend Only if there is more than one series; better: label lines directly Colour One highlight colour for the point you want to make, grey for the rest Template Right-click → Save as Template to reuse your style The core chart types Here are the core charts built from Superstore data. The Excel steps follow each one. Column (comparison), line (trend), clustered and stacked bar (comparison of parts), and scatter (relationship). Chart Excel path Use when Clustered column / bar Insert → Column or Bar Comparing categories; use bar when labels are long or "}, {"i": "excel-11", "t": "Interactive Dashboard: Superstore Case Study", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-11-interactive-dashboard-superstore-case-study.html", "s": " The brief Case study Superstore sales review The Head of Sales wants a one-page Excel dashboard to review performance for 2023–2025. They want to see, at a glance: total sales, profit, profit margin and orders; how sales are trending; which regions, categories and sub-categories perform best and worst; and the effect of discounts on profit. They want to filter by year, region and segment. The dashboard will be emailed every month, so it must be protected against accidental changes. Step 0: questions and KPIs first KPI / question Calculation Visual Total sales Sum of sales KPI card Total profit and margin Sum of profit; profit ÷ sales KPI cards Number of orders Distinct count of order_id KPI card Sales trend Sales by month Line chart Regional performance Sales and profit by region Bar chart Product mix Sales by category and sub-category Bar chart / treemap Discount impact Profit by discount level Column chart Filters Year, Region, Segment Timeline + slicers Agreeing on this table before building saves hours of rework. It is also your \"requirements document\" in interviews. Here is the finished result we are aiming for (drawn from the real data): The finished Superstore dashboard: KPI cards on top, trend and breakdown charts below, slicers on the left. Step 1: prepare the data Import superstore_sales.csv with Data → From Text/CSV (Power Query, Lesson 12), or open it and convert the range to a Table named tblSales ( Ctrl + T ). Add helper columns that the pivots will need: Colum"}, {"i": "excel-12", "t": "Power Query, Power Pivot, Analysis ToolPak & What-If Analysis", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-12-power-query-power-pivot-analysis-toolpak-and-what-if-analysi.html", "s": " Why \"Power\" tools? Formulas and pivots are great until the same cleaning has to be repeated every month, the data has several related tables, or it grows past a million rows. The Power tools solve exactly these problems, and they are the same engines used in Power BI , so everything you learn here transfers directly. The modern Excel analytics stack. Power Query and the Data Model are shared with Power BI. Power Query: repeatable cleaning Power Query (Data → Get &amp; Transform Data ) imports data and records every cleaning step you click. Next month, put the new file in place and click Refresh : all steps run again automatically. Step by step Your first query Data → Get Data → From File → From Text/CSV → choose superstore_sales.csv → Transform Data . The Power Query Editor opens. Check data types in each column header (ABC = text, 123 = whole number, 1.2 = decimal, calendar = date). Fix any wrong ones (right-click header → Change Type). Remove columns you do not need (select → right-click → Remove, or Choose Columns ). Filter rows with the header drop-downs (for example remove test orders). Add a column: Add Column → Date → Year , or Custom Column with a formula such as [sales] - [profit] . Rename the query (right panel) to Sales → Home → Close &amp; Load To… → Table, PivotTable report, or Only Create Connection + Add to Data Model . The Power Query Editor. Every action you take is recorded in Applied Steps on the right. Transformations you will use most Transformation Wher"}];